<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Djurdjura</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Djurdjura Mountains</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Djurdjura is a mountain range of the Tell <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, rising to 2,308 m at Lalla Khedidja and forming the backbone of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-kabylia">Greater Kabylia</a>. Its villages were the base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cabyle">Kabyle</a> kingdom of Kuku in the 16th and 17th centuries and long resisted outside rule, until the French conquered the region in 1857. Part of the range is protected as Djurdjura National Park.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/djurdjura.jpg" alt="Djurdjura" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Melb13 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Djurjura,_Algeria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
